  "worked_solution": "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} & =k \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=2$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
